    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d28">

                <pb facs="0176" n="48" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg48"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 28.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Count Kalnoky to
                        Baron Schaeffer</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation of telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-05-08"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 8,
                        1885</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>We regret the nomination of Mr. Keiley as minister plenipotentiary and envoy
                    extraordinary to the imperial court and his sudden departure from America, as
                    here, too, like in Rome, <hi rendition="#italic">prevail scruples against this
                        choice.</hi></p>
                <p>Please direct <hi rendition="#italic">in the most friendly way</hi> the attention
                    of the American Government to the generally existing diplomatic practice to ask
                    previously to any nomination of a foreign minister the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >agrément</hi> (consent) of the Government to which he is accredited.</p>
                <p>You are therefore requested to <hi rendition="#italic">earnestly entreat</hi>
                    them that the newly nominated minister may not reach Vienna before our
                    confidential consent to his nomination has taken place.</p>
                <p>The position of a foreign envoy wedded to a Jewess by civil marriage would be
                    untenable and even impossible in Vienna.</p>
                <closer><signed>COUNT KALNOKY.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
